Intern Graduation Panel — Moderator Script

OPENING

[Walk on stage, smile, pause for a moment before starting.]

«“Success is not only about how far you’ve come, but also about remembering where you started.”»

Good evening, everyone!

And firstly, congratulations to all our graduates sitting here today.

Today is obviously a special day for our interns who are making that transition from being interns to becoming full-time employees.

But I have to admit—this panel is slightly personal for me as well.

Because not too long ago, I was sitting exactly where they are today.

My own journey here started through Innoversite, with a 24-hour hackathon.

And anyone who has participated in a 24-hour hackathon knows that after a certain point, you’re not really sure whether your solution is getting better…

or the lack of sleep is simply making it look better.

[Pause for laughter.]

That hackathon eventually led to a six-month internship, and the internship led to a full-time role.

And today, almost a year later, I get to sit on the other side and moderate a conversation with people who have just completed a very similar journey.

So I promise this is not going to be an interview.

You’ve already cleared enough of those.

Today, I want to talk about the things that usually don’t make it onto a résumé—

the nervous first days, the mistakes, the people who helped, the moments when something finally clicked, and those experiences where you suddenly realized:

“Okay… college definitely did not prepare me for this.”

So let's hear the story behind the conversion, and not just the conversion itself.

---

1. LAXMI — THE HACKATHON

Laxmi, I think it’s fitting that we start right where the journey started for many of us—the hackathon.

I remember during my Innoversite experience, those 24 hours felt like a strange combination of coding competition, brainstorming session, caffeine experiment and survival challenge.

So take us back to your experience.

What was the hackathon like for you?

And more importantly, was there any particular moment during those 24 hours when you thought:

“Okay, this might actually work!”

—or perhaps—

“What have I gotten myself into?”

[After her answer]

That’s the interesting thing about hackathons.

We usually remember the final presentation and the result, but behind that are probably 20 ideas that didn't work, three last-minute fixes and at least one moment of complete panic.

QUICK AUDIENCE CONNECT

Actually, quick show of hands—

How many people here have participated in a hackathon before?

[Look around / acknowledge hands.]

And keep your hands up if at some point during that hackathon you seriously questioned your life choices.

[Pause for reaction.]

There we go. Now we have an honest audience.

---

2. DEEPSHITHA — CAMPUS TO CORPORATE: ONBOARDING & TRAINING

The hackathon may get you through the door, but then comes a completely different challenge:

Campus to Corporate.

Deepshitha, when you're in college, you know your professors, your timetable, your exams, and roughly what is expected from you.

Then suddenly you enter corporate life and start hearing terms, acronyms, processes and meeting invitations that you have absolutely no context for.

And for the first few weeks, sometimes you just nod confidently and make a mental note:

“I need to Google this later.”

[Pause.]

So, how was that transition for you?

How did the onboarding and training help you move from being a student to actually feeling comfortable in a professional environment?

OPTIONAL FOLLOW-UP

And do you remember the point when you stopped feeling like:

“I'm the new intern here…”

and started feeling:

“Okay, I actually belong here and know what I'm doing.”

---

3. LAXMI — CAMPUS TO CORPORATE

Laxmi, let me bring you back in here.

Deepshitha has spoken about onboarding and training, but everyone experiences this transition differently.

So from your perspective:

What was the biggest adjustment you personally had to make while moving from campus life to corporate life?

Was it communication? Ownership? Time management? Working with different people?

Or simply realizing that unlike college…

there is no professor announcing that the deadline has been extended by two days?

[Pause for laughter.]

AUDIENCE MOMENT

Actually, I want the graduates in the audience to think about this too.

Think back to your first week here.

Now compare that person with who you are today.

Same person…

probably much better Outlook calendar management.

[Smile.]

That difference is probably one of the best ways to measure what this internship has actually taught you.

---

4. SHRAVYA — EYE-OPENING MOMENTS ABOUT IT

Now let's talk about something I find particularly interesting.

We study technology for years before entering the industry.

But understanding technology academically and seeing how technology works inside a large organization can be two very different things.

Shravya, during your internship, what was your biggest eye-opening moment about working in IT?

Was there something you encountered where you thought:

“Wow, I had absolutely no idea this is how things work in the real world.”

AFTER HER ANSWER

I think that's something almost everyone in technology eventually discovers.

In college:

“It works on my laptop.”

Sounds like a perfectly reasonable argument.

In production…

not quite as convincing.

[Pause for laughter.]

And you start realizing that writing the code is sometimes only one part of solving the actual problem.

---

5. BHAVYA — SUPPORT FROM MANAGER

One thing we haven't spoken about yet is the people who make this transition easier.

Because internships aren't shaped only by projects or technology.

Very often, they're shaped by people.

Bhavya, I'd like to talk about the support you received from your manager.

When you're an intern, asking questions can sometimes be intimidating.

You don't want to ask too many questions because you want to look independent…

but you also don't want to ask too few questions and spend three hours confidently solving the wrong problem.

[Smile.]

So how did the support and guidance from your manager help you during your internship?

And was there any particular advice or feedback that has stayed with you?

AUDIENCE / MANAGER ACKNOWLEDGEMENT

And I think this is also a good moment to acknowledge something.

Behind many of the graduates sitting here today, there are managers, mentors, buddies and teammates who took time to answer questions, review work, give feedback and sometimes simply say:

“Don't worry. Try again.”

So I think they deserve a round of applause as well.

[Gesture toward managers/mentors and lead applause.]

---

6. SHRAVYA — CROSS-FUNCTIONAL CONNECTIONS

Shravya, coming back to you—

One thing that changes very quickly after college is the kind of people you work with.

In college, most of the people around you are studying roughly the same subjects and are at roughly the same stage.

At work, suddenly you're collaborating with developers, business teams, managers, support teams, people from different functions—and sometimes different countries and cultures.

So how important were those cross-functional connections during your internship?

Did interacting with people outside your immediate team change the way you understood either the organization or your own role?

FOLLOW-UP / QUIRK

And most importantly—

how long did it take before you realized that half of corporate communication is figuring out who exactly needs to be CC'd?

[Pause.]

I'm still learning that one.

---

7. BHAVYA — REAL-WORLD PROJECTS

And Bhavya, let's finish the main discussion with perhaps the biggest difference between college and corporate life:

Real-world projects.

In college, if your project doesn't work, worst case scenario…

you lose some marks.

In the corporate world, suddenly there are users, deadlines, dependencies and actual consequences attached to what you're building.

So tell us about the experience of working on a real-world project.

What changed when you realized:

“Someone is actually going to use what I'm working on”?

Did that change the way you approached responsibility, quality or problem-solving?

---

RAPID-FIRE ROUND

Before we finish, I think we've been far too serious.

So let's do a quick rapid-fire round.

No corporate answers allowed.

I'll ask each of you one. First thing that comes to mind.

One word to describe your internship?

One thing you wish somebody had told you on Day 1?

One skill you discovered was far more important than you expected?

One person—or one type of person—you would definitely recommend every intern connects with?

And finally:

If you could send one WhatsApp message to yourself on the night before your internship started, what would it say?

[Let the answers breathe—this section can generate some of the best spontaneous moments.]

