AUDIENCE ENGAGEMENT

Before we close, I want to involve everyone here.

Especially those who have completed their internship journey today.

I want you to think of one person who made your internship easier.

It could be your manager.

Your mentor.

Your buddy.

A teammate.

Or maybe another intern who was equally confused as you were during Week 1.

[Pause.]

You don't have to say their name out loud.

But sometime after this ceremony, go and tell them.

Because while today celebrates your achievement, journeys like these are very rarely completed alone.

---

CLOSING

I want to end with something that I think connects my journey with theirs.

A year ago, I was on their side of this journey.

Today, I'm moderating their graduation panel.

And perhaps a year from now, one of them will be standing here having this conversation with the next batch.

That's what makes today interesting.

Graduation sounds like an ending.

But professionally, this is almost exactly the opposite.

The word “intern” disappears from your designation.

But hopefully, the curiosity that came with being an intern doesn't disappear with it.

Keep asking questions.

Keep volunteering for things you don't completely know how to do yet.

Keep making connections outside your comfort zone.

And keep that little bit of nervousness you had on your first day—because it usually means you're still doing something that challenges you.

To Laxmi, Deepshitha, Shravya and Bhavya, congratulations on completing this chapter and beginning the next one.

And to everyone graduating today—

Welcome to the other side.

Trust me…

the learning has only just begun.

Thank you, everyone.

[Applause / transition to next segment.]